# `node_modules` without a trailing slash on purpose.
#
# The slash makes the pattern match *directories only*, so a **symlink** named
# `node_modules` was not ignored — and this repository had one committed, pointing
# at a harness install that no longer exists:
#
#   $ git ls-files -s node_modules
#   120000 f8d42d89a377dc3d95711046d86244e96ef6deb2 0	node_modules
#   $ git cat-file -p f8d42d8
#   /mnt/c/BL/AI/DSH Desktop/resources/app/node_modules
#
# Two consequences, both measured: `git status` permanently showed ` D node_modules`,
# and a single `git restore` would recreate that dead link in the repository root —
# where a symlink called `node_modules` misleads npm and pnpm.
#
# `node_modules` matches both the directory and the symlink, which is what is wanted.
node_modules
.refs/
*.log
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.tgz
